  "summary": "The T-shaped wrapped robe and its seasonal language: pattern, obi and occasion encoded on silk.",
  "description": "Kimono dress codes turn bolt-straight silk into a social grammar: yuzen-dyed furisode for unmarried women's fluttering sleeves, homongi for visits, tomesode with hem-only patterns for married formality, seasonal motifs (plum for New Year, maple for autumn) placed to be read in motion. The wide obi belt — brocaded, stiffened, tied in sculptural knots — anchors the composition, while layering (kasane) and collar spacing signal age and taste. Modern designers from Issey Miyake to global runways keep re-draping its logic.",
